event('195-C1-Picard-tangent','targeted proof check','For the split dual-number thickening V_epsilon of V, the multiplicative kernel 1+epsilon O_V is additively O_V. The split map on global units makes the kernel of Pic(V_epsilon) to Pic(V) equal to H^1(V,O_V). For a representing Picard scheme at its rational identity, maps to k[epsilon] reducing to that identity identify the tangent space with Hom_k(m/m^2,k), proving the asserted canonical tangent identification. Finite type then gives equality of tangent dimension and local dimension exactly at a regular identity; at a rational point this is smoothness over k, and group translations propagate smoothness. A finite local nontrivial group scheme can have one geometric point and nonzero tangent space: alpha_p has algebra k[t]/(t^p), comultiplication t to t tensor 1 plus 1 tensor t, dimension zero and one-dimensional cotangent space. This is an illustrative calculation, not an identification of Igusa\'s particular Picard scheme.','195, p. 380, Igusa/Picard paragraph')
event('195-C1-unframed-vector-bundles','targeted proof check','The warning about vector-bundle moduli has a concrete local mechanism. On a smooth projective genus-one curve with H^1(O)=k, first-order deformations of O^2 with a fixed special-fiber identification are H^1(End(O^2))=M_2(k), by the linear Cech transition-cocycle calculation. Forgetting that identification takes conjugacy orbits under GL_2(k). Over k plus a two-dimensional square-zero ideal it takes simultaneous conjugacy orbits of pairs. The pairs (D,D) and (D,E), with D=diag(1,0) and E=diag(0,1), are not simultaneously conjugate, but their two separate conjugacy classes agree. Thus this isomorphism-class functor fails preservation of the fiber product of two dual-number algebras over k, obstructing pro-representability. This concerns the unframed functor, and does not rule out framed functors or other suitably restricted moduli problems.','195, p. 380, failure of pro-representability for vector-bundle classification')
event('195-C1-formal-to-global-prospect','carried as background without fault found','The three alternatives distinguish failure of the Artinian restriction, infinite-dimensional tangent spaces, and noetherian completed local rings. The text explicitly describes the last alternative as a strong presumption and proposes additional global properties for future work; it is not a claimed proof of algebraization. The two occurrences of bien representable are retained as printed in the topological-algebra discussion rather than silently changed to pro-representable. The specific historical Igusa surface is not constructed on this page and remains background pending contextual work.','195, p. 380, middle paragraph and historical example')
record_page('195',380,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/195.tex entry lines 546–597 and sources/fr/fr/195.tex entry lines 547–598 with scan leaf 13 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands. Read the opening X subscript, both tangent-test fibers, every occurrence of H^1 and m/m-squared, and the Hom/Pi/Aut heading.

Both layers restore the hyphen in quasi-complètes, printed plural telles, reference punctuation, ordinary quotes, spaced abbreviations, inline H^1 inequality, wavy O/Hom/Aut, roman underlined heading and upright variables. Printed French restores O_X and the stray point after on a; corrected French retains O_Y and the uninterrupted sentence, with two explicit rows. The printed shorthand bien représentable is retained.

Checked the Picard tangent space using the split dual-number unit sequence, the smoothness/dimension comparison at the rational identity, and an alpha_p illustration of a one-point scheme with nonzero tangent space. Gave an explicit simultaneous-conjugacy failure for unframed rank-two vector-bundle deformations. The proposed passage from formal data to global representability is recorded as a prospect, and the particular Igusa construction as background.''')
